// A declaration-head generic clause is what makes a nested ADT unmistakable: no
// field declarator takes one, so this cannot be read as a field.
//
// The UNPARAMETERIZED spelling is deliberately not the case here. `Shape
// co.data = …;` in a class body is an initialized FIELD, because
// `class-member` offers field-declaration and no data-declaration at all —
// `accepted/carrier-typed-fields` pins that. A unit body reads the same tokens as
// a data-declaration, but the two member grammars are disjoint rather than
// competing: `unit-member` has data-declaration and no field-declaration. Each
// body has exactly one matching production.
_ co.class = {

    Shape(T) co.data = Some(T) | None;
}
